      Ankle &amp; Foot Pain
     </span>
    </li>
   </ol>
  </nav>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
   {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://ryansrolfing.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Specialties"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Ankle & Foot Pain"}]}
  </script>

  <article class="px-5 py-20 md:py-28">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.28em] text-[#c4924a]">
     Ankle and foot pain
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 text-5xl leading-tight md:text-7xl">
     Rolfing for Ankle &amp; Foot Pain in Folsom
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
     The feet meet the ground for you all day. When they are stiff, collapsed, or still guarding an old sprain, the knees, hips, and low back often pick up the extra work.
    </p>
   </div>
   <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-12 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      More than the sore spot
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Foot and ankle pain can follow a sprain that never quite finished, the surfaces you stand on, or a habit of rolling to the outside or inside of the foot. The local ache matters. So does whether the lower leg, knee, and hip can let the foot adapt.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing includes the feet because the rest of the body stands on them. A low back or knee pattern sometimes quiets only after the foot has a clearer job.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What a session may include
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Ryan may work with the foot, ankle, calf, and the hip or pelvis that has been compensating. Standing and a few steps are part of seeing whether the foot can take weight without the rest of you bracing. Pressure on the foot can be specific. It should still be something you can stay with.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      The goal is not to mash a tender sole until it gives up. It is to help the foot, ankle, and leg share the work of walking.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What this work does not do
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing is not podiatry or medical care. It does not treat fractures, stress fractures, infections, gout flares, or diabetic foot problems, and it will not rebuild a torn ligament. A hot, swollen, or newly injured ankle or foot needs a medical look before bodywork.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      For recurring stiffness after an old sprain, or feet that seem to feed knee and back tension, Rolfing can be a practical way to work with the pattern. It is not a promise that the pain will resolve.
     </p>
    </section>
   </div>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl rounded-[2rem] bg-[#545454] p-8 text-white md:p-10">
    <h2 class="text-4xl text-white">
     This work may fit if you notice
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-7 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-white/82">
     <li>
      Ankle or foot stiffness that lingers after an old sprain.
     </li>
     <li>
      Feet that feel stuck, collapsed, or braced by the end of the day.
     </li>
     <li>
      Knee, hip, or low back tension that seems to start from how you stand.
     </li>
     <li>
      Temporary relief from stretching or massage, then the same pattern returns.
     </li>
    </ul>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight">
     Questions people ask
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Can Rolfing help ankle or foot pain?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Rolfing may support some people with recurring foot and ankle strain by working with the foot, lower leg, and how weight travels up through the knee, hip, and back. It is not medical or podiatric treatment.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Do you work on the feet themselves?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Yes, when the feet are part of the pattern. Sessions also look above the ankle, because a guarded foot often changes the whole leg.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       What if I have plantar fasciitis or an old sprain?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Those labels cover a range of problems. Ryan does not diagnose them. If the issue is recurring and you are medically cleared, sessions can look at the foot and the way you load it. A new injury, tear, or stress fracture is a medical question first.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       When should I see a doctor first?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Seek medical care first for a foot or ankle that is hot, very swollen, bruised after an injury, numb, or too painful to bear weight, and for wounds, signs of infection, or pain that is sudden or worsening.
      </p>
     </details>
    </div>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
     Ankle and foot bodywork in Folsom
    </h2>
    <p>
     Ryan’s Rolfing is located at 101 Parkshore Drive, Folsom, CA 95630, and serves clients from Folsom, El Dorado Hills, Shingle Springs, Cameron Park, Placerville, and the greater Sacramento area.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full bg-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-white hover:bg-[#9b7139]" href="https://rolfing-106793.square.site">
      Book a session
     </a>
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full border border-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[#c4924a] hover:bg-[#f8f6f2]" href="/rolfing-for-knee-pain-folsom/">
      Knee pain support
     </a>
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full border border-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[#c4924a] hover:bg-[#f8f6f2]" href="/serving/folsom-rolfing/">
      Folsom office
     </a>
    </div>
    <p>
     Related:
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-back-pain-folsom/">low back pain</a>,
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-sciatica-folsom/">sciatica</a>,
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-neck-pain-folsom/">neck pain</a>.
    </p>
   </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
